6}, "rating_margin": -7.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.67, "baseline_margin": -14.93, "analyst_adjustment": 2.93, "key_factors": ["Begich is a well-funded incumbent in an R+6 state with $1.8M on hand", "Democratic nominee Eric Hafner is in federal prison, limiting his campaign", "Raters split between Lean R and Likely R, all favoring Begich", "Independent Bill Hill has raised $2.6M and could siphon anti-incumbent votes"], "rationale": "Begich combines incumbency, a large fundraising edge, and a favorable partisan lean in Alaska, while the Democratic nominee's imprisonment makes a competitive race implausible. The raw polling average is misleading because Hafner is barely known; fundamentals and the ratings consensus point to a double-digit Republican win, with Hill's independent bid the main source of uncertainty.", "watch": "Whether Bill Hill's well-funded independent campaign consolidates anti-incumbent and Democratic-leaning votes enough under ranked-choice voting to threaten Begich.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Nick Begich III (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Nick Begich III seeks a full term in Alaska's at-large House seat after advancing from the primary. He faces Democrat Eric Hafner, who is running from federal prison, independent Bill Hill, and Libertarian Jim McDermott. Begich has raised $6.4 million and holds a large cash advantage, while the district's partisan lean favors Republicans by six points.\n\nPolls consistently show Begich well ahead, though the Democratic nominee's low profile makes those numbers hard to interpret precisely. Most race raters call the contest Lean or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats almost no chance. The main wildcard is Hill, who has raised substantial money and could attract anti-incumbent voters in a ranked-choice tabulation, but Begich remains the strong favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1234, "margin": -7.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23"